   "format": "short_answer",
   "stem": {
      "text": "The function s is given by s(x) = (x + 3) / (x^2 + 5x - 6) for -4 <= x <= 6. Consider the largest interval that contains x = 0 and on which s is continuous. What is the sum of the integers in that interval?"
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "numeric",
      "mathjson": -10
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "The denominator x^2 + 5x - 6 = (x + 6)(x - 1) is zero at x = -6 and x = 1. Only x = 1 lies in the stated domain -4 <= x <= 6, so the domain of s is [-4, 1) U (1, 6].",
         "mathjson": [
            "Multiply",
            [
               "Add",
               "x",
               6
            ],
            [
               "Subtract",
               "x",
               1
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "s is a rational function, continuous at every input of its domain, with one sided continuity at the stated endpoints -4 and 6, so s is continuous on [-4, 1) and on (1, 6].",
         "mathjson": [
            "Divide",
            [
               "Add",
               "x",
               3
            ],
            [
               "Multiply",
               [
                  "Add",
                  "x",
                  6
               ],
               [
                  "Subtract",
                  "x",
                  1
               ]
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "The piece that contains x = 0 is [-4, 1), closed at -4 because s(-4) exists and s is continuous from the right there, and open at 1. Its integers are -4, -3, -2, -1 and 0.",
         "mathjson": [
            "Add",
            -4,
            -3,
            -2,
            -1,
            0
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "The sum of those integers is -10.",
         "mathjson": -10
      }
   ],
